<p>Spirit Airlines said it was going out of business after 34 years and began an “orderly wind-down” effective immediately, canceling all flights, according to the company’s restructuring website and reports carrying its announcement. The U.S. budget carrier employed about 17,000 people and once operated hundreds of daily flights on its bright yellow aircraft. The shutdown followed the collapse of talks over a proposed $500 million government lifeline from the Trump administration for a carrier that had entered bankruptcy proceedings for the second time in less than two years. President Trump said Friday that the White House had given Spirit and its creditors a final proposal, while reports said a board meeting ended without an agreement. Spirit had struggled since the Covid-19 pandemic with higher operating costs, debt and lost revenue; one account said losses had exceeded $2.5 billion since 2020. Reports said jet fuel prices linked by sources to the Iran war added to the latest pressure.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Aj4K6rG6Pkg5f9Qo-v7HC0GTmax7y30HA-amZds2ZgO4QteIqLK-Si4yn10kl165fkZPlvhZmqXsjOrwhuiGaOI7tyTp4Zhu4cpJcQ_cdl5UgQjUpGK2qQ5AKgdeWQWR3D_cnoNhLpw' alt='A Spirit Airlines airplane at Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International Airport in Florida.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cash crunch: CBS reported before Spirit’s final announcement that the airline was essentially out of available cash and could cease operations as soon as Saturday without last-minute intervention.</li><li>Ownership concern: Fast Company reported that investors balked at the prospect of the federal government becoming a majority owner under the rescue plan.</li><li>Political pushback: Reuters reported that the proposed taxpayer rescue faced opposition from some of Trump’s advisers and many Republicans in Congress.</li><li>Shutdown timing: Reuters sources said before the announcement that Spirit was preparing to cease operations around 3 a.m. on May 2.</li></ul><blockquote>If we could do it, we’d do it, but only if it’s a good deal - President Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spirit Airlines: The company said it was proud of the impact of its ultra-low-cost model and had hoped to keep serving guests, calling the closure the end of a model it said reshaped the industry. (<a href='https://www.spiritrestructuring.com/'>Spirit restructuring website</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump suggested before the talks failed that the government might be able to resell the airline for a profit once oil prices driven up by the Iran war came down. (<a href='https://www.stuff.co.nz/travel/360973331/trump-says-final-proposal-rescue-spirit-airlines-under-consideration'>Stuff</a>)</li><li>American Airlines: American Airlines said before the shutdown that it was capping main-cabin fares for Spirit routes to cities where it also offered nonstop service. (<a href='https://www.nbcboston.com/news/national-international/spirit-airlines-bailout-shut-down/3943237/'>NBC Boston</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/business/Industry/spirit-airlines-appears-closer-to-shutdown-as-time-dwindles-for-government-bailout/article70930780.ece'>Spirit Airlines says it’s going out of business after 34 years and is ending operations</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/18ab5a6a-397a-47b5-af06-f8e846b45bf1'>Spirit Airlines winds down operations after bailout talks founder</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/spirit-airlines-shuts-down-industry-s-first-iran-war-casualty'>Spirit Airlines shuts down, industry’s first Iran war casualty</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/050126-the-takeout-with-major-garrett/'>5/1: The Takeout with Major Garrett</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/spirit-airlines-says-they-are-going-out-of-business/a-77016867'>Spirit Airlines says they are going out of business</a> - dw.com</li><